{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Then Abraham fell upon his face.</b> He already mentioned this above — ״ויפל אברם על פניו״ — but that one was to direct his mind toward prophecy, whereas this one was to pray for Ishmael." "<b>And the fact</b> that Scripture mentions regarding him the expression of falling each and every time, is to teach you that before the circumcision he would not prophesy except while falling, because the foreskin is abhorrent before the Holy One, blessed be He; and so we find regarding Balaam, concerning whom it is written (Numbers 24:4): ״נופל וגלוי עינים״ — falling and with uncovered eyes. But after he was circumcised, he would prophesy standing, as it is written (Genesis 18:22): ״ואברהם עודנו עומד לפני ה׳״." "<b>Shall a man of a hundred years beget?</b> Heaven forfend that Abraham should find the matter too far-fetched, by way of miracle and wonder." "<b>Rather,</b> according to the way of nature he spoke and said: <b>shall a man of a hundred</b> who did not beget in his youth from this woman — shall he beget now? <b>And if Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear,</b> after her flow has ceased from her — this is impossible in any manner by way of nature. But by way of miracle and wonder he knew and believed that it is possible through the power of the Creator, blessed be He, as Scripture testified concerning him above: ״והאמין בה׳״ — and he believed in HaShem." "And as for the fact that Scripture mentions concerning him <b>ויצחק</b> — this is not laughter and mockery, heaven forfend, but an expression of joy, in the manner of (Psalms 126:2): ״אז ימלא שחוק פינו״ — then our mouth shall be filled with laughter. And therefore Onkelos translated it as וחדי — 'and he rejoiced' — and did not translate וחייך. And the proof that the expression ויצחק is from the language of joy is that he was commanded by the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, to call him יצחק, as it is stated: ״וקראת את שמו יצחק״ — and you shall call his name Isaac. And if Abraham had mocked the matter, the Holy One, blessed be He, would not have commanded him to call him by a name commemorating mockery; but certainly, since He so commanded him, Abraham's intention was only for good."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:58.613491644Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}